    {
      "id": 223869,
      "name": "Phantom5613",
      "username": "Phantom5613",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantom5613/{size}/1906_2.png",
      "created_at": "2018-11-11T21:20:43.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Trajector\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/trajector/48/1914_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Trajector:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I agree, though I didn’t like this particular story that much. It seemed like they came up with a framing for scenes that threw all the horror tropes at the wall to see what stuck, instead of having a focused story. Only one of the scenes in the house was actually necessary - the one where Nightmist discovers her client’s husband - the rest could be cut.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I respectfully disagree. The story itself was never really about the mystery of what happened to the victim, but about the&nbsp;multiple stories&nbsp;of the house and it’s dark history. True, they used alot of tropes, but that doesn’t mean that they were haphazard in their use. They all helped create a very creepy atmosphere, like the house itself was collecting horror stories and storing them in all the different rooms inside itself…</p>",
      "post_number": 6,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:57:07.901Z",
